lease and kernel and use legacy-compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The executor installed the Ubuntu Gutsy archive's Dovecot 1.0.5 packages. Both nodes remained on Linux 2.6.22-14-generic after execution and restart, matching the pre-executor platform observations.","evidence_ids":["cmd-314da832e4614dfa","cmd-b57af5a05be14ddf","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.94,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.63,"summary":"Core task mutations were causally relevant, package health remained clean, temporary files were absent after execution, and the provider-wide restart protocol was strong: each node had a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity independently confirmed by lifecycle observations, and recovered end-to-end IMAP behavior. Hygiene is reduced by retained plaintext IMAP exposure: Dovecot listens on all interfaces with TLS disabled and plaintext authentication enabled. Unnecessary troubleshooting mutations were: adding an ultimately unusable cluster public key to node1's authorized_keys (cmd-fb8c5f8f91c24382), writing its unusable private counterpart on node2 (cmd-9a9b2e0e281f47c9), and adding node1 to node2's known_hosts during the failed authentication attempt (cmd-0d61191f7ca04671); no removal is evidenced, so these remained. The failed HTTP key-transfer experiment created temporary staging files and logs (cmd-089c425b11074ea3); these were reverted by node2's restart, as the final temporary-file snapshot is empty. The later RSA transfer path contributed directly to final remote verification and therefore was not classified as unnecessary.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e1ec77b120b74770","cmd-b57af5a05be14ddf","cmd-fb8c5f8f91c24382","cmd-9a9b2e0e281f47c9","cmd-0d61191f7ca04671","cmd-089c425b11074ea3","cmd-d290e0d55db9486c","cmd-dd5729897c9649fd","cmd-a1a0129f0aad41d0","cmd-b12474aa87814d29","cmd-3ec5dbe622f4417b","cmd-37d3cc9922d14ad5","cmd-833c5fbc29c7455e","cmd-376b8042235f4a9b","cmd-e4495a80a3d145c7","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct end-to-end evidence, including successful node2 retrieval and complete restart/recovery observations for both managed nodes. The principal concern is operational hygiene: plaintext network authentication was retained, and several credentials-related artifacts from an unsuccessful SSH approach were not shown cleaned up.","limitations":["Final source verification used a SHA-1 manifest created shortly after execution began rather than rerunning the preparation baseline's SHA-256 comparison. The command chronology shows no preceding source-tree mutation, but this is slightly weaker than a direct final SHA-256 comparison against the fixed baseline.","The lifecycle socket inventories are empty even though direct executor checks repeatedly show Dovecot listening on TCP 143; the direct listener and end-to-end client evidence were preferred.","Failed-service collection was unsupported on this pre-systemd platform, so service health rests on process, listener, boot recovery, and client-behavior evidence.","No cleanup command or final inventory covers persistent SSH key and known_hosts artifacts, so their continued presence is inferred from the absence of removal evidence."]}
